  "content": {
    "id": "p124-practice-06-question",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "For what values of $c$ will $|ax+b|=c$ have the following number of solutions?",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-06-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "One.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $a\\ne0$: $c=0$.\n\nFor $a=0$: no such value of $c$.",
          "worked": "For $a\\ne0$, the absolute value is zero at exactly one value of $x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}|ax+b|&=0\\\\ax+b&=0\\\\ax&=-b\\\\x&=-\\frac{b}{a}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $c=0$ gives exactly one solution. Positive $c$ gives two distinct solutions, while negative $c$ gives none.\n\nFor $a=0$, the equation becomes $|b|=c$. Every real $x$ is a solution when $c=|b|$, and there are no solutions otherwise. No value of $c$ gives exactly one solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-06-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Two.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $a\\ne0$: $c>0$.\n\nFor $a=0$: no such value of $c$.",
          "worked": "For $a\\ne0$ and $c>0$, the expression inside the absolute value can equal either $c$ or $-c$:\n$$\\begin{align*}ax+b&=\\pm c\\\\ax&=-b\\pm c\\\\x&=\\frac{-b\\pm c}{a}.\\end{align*}$$\nThese are two distinct solutions because $c>0$. When $c=0$, they coincide; negative $c$ gives no solution.\n\nFor $a=0$, the equation becomes $|b|=c$. It has every real $x$ as a solution when $c=|b|$, and no solutions otherwise. No value of $c$ gives exactly two solutions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-06-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "None.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $a\\ne0$: $c<0$.\n\nFor $a=0$: $c\\ne|b|$.",
          "worked": "An absolute value is nonnegative, so $c<0$ gives no solutions.\n\nFor $a\\ne0$, every $c\\ge0$ gives at least one solution: $c=0$ gives one, and $c>0$ gives two. Therefore there are no solutions exactly when $c<0$.\n\nFor $a=0$, the equation becomes $|b|=c$. It has no solutions when $c\\ne|b|$. When $c=|b|$, every real $x$ is a solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
